2022 ruler conduct and inherited posture, where Marcos actually made or repeated choices:
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Executive Order No. 164","publisher":"LawPhil","publication_date":"2022-02-28","url":"https://lawphil.net/executive/execord/eo2022/eo_164_2022.html","claim":"Before Marcos took office, the Duterte administration adopted a national position for a nuclear energy program and required IAEA-guided planning, stakeholder consultation, and review of the Bataan Nuclear Power Plant.","locator":"EO 164, sections and whereas clauses on national position, IAEA guidelines, and BNPP review","source_type":"legal","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary legal text that sets the inherited civilian nuclear-policy baseline.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Pre-Marcos inherited baseline from February 2022, still shaping 2022 policy space.","ruler_attribution":"Not Marcos-authored; it is inherited context that Marcos continued working within.","contrary_evidence":["Because it predates Marcos's inauguration, it cannot be counted as his own 2022 decision."],"lenses":["1B.3","1B.7","1B.9","1B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"State of the Nation Address of His Excellency Ferdinand R. Marcos Jr. President of the Philippines to the Congress of the Philippines","publisher":"Presidential Communications Office","publication_date":"2022-07-25","url":"https://pco.gov.ph/presidential-speech/state-of-the-nation-address-of-his-excellency-ferdinand-r-marcos-jr-president-of-the-philippines-to-the-congress-of-the-philippines/","claim":"In his first SONA, Marcos said it was time to re-examine the Philippines' strategy toward nuclear power plants and said the country would comply with IAEA regulations for nuclear power plants.","locator":"SONA energy section on nuclear power and IAEA compliance","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary presidential speech text setting Marcos's own 2022 policy framing.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 ruler-period evidence.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to Marcos's public policy stance in office.","contrary_evidence":["This is still a strategy statement, not a completed safety, oversight, or deployment decision.","Environment and safety critics remained unconvinced that nuclear was the best path."],"lenses":["1B.3","1B.4","1B.7","1B.9","1B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"'We will find a way': Marcos says on energy concerns","publisher":"Philstar.com","publication_date":"2022-06-30","url":"https://www.philstar.com/headlines/2022/06/30/2192058/we-will-find-way-marcos-says-energy-concerns","claim":"On taking office, Marcos acknowledged a power-supply dilemma, said he had no detailed plan in the inaugural speech, and tied energy choices to the broader supply shock caused by the Ukraine war and domestic fuel stress.","locator":"June 30, 2022 report on post-inauguration remarks","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Independent contemporaneous reporting with direct quotation from Marcos.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Exact early-term evidence from the day Marcos entered office.","ruler_attribution":"Directly about Marcos's first-day framing, but mediated by reporter narration.","contrary_evidence":["The article also notes that environment groups raised safety and waste-disposal concerns about nuclear power.","It does not show any concrete nuclear-risk-reduction action."],"lenses":["1B.1","1B.9","1B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"PBBM seeks peaceful resolutions on NoKor, Russia-Ukraine crisis","publisher":"Philippine News Agency","publication_date":"2022-11-11","url":"https://www.pna.gov.ph/articles/1188356","claim":"Marcos called for peaceful resolution of North Korea's nuclear threat and urged adherence to UN Security Council resolutions, which is the clearest 2022 de-escalatory nuclear-related statement in the record.","locator":"ASEAN Summit Retreat intervention on North Korea","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Government newswire reporting Marcos's intervention and its nuclear-related content.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 ruler-period evidence.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to Marcos's foreign-policy messaging.","contrary_evidence":["This is diplomatic rhetoric, not a verified structural change in command or safeguards.","The source does not show direct leverage over any nuclear arsenal."],"lenses":["1B.2","1B.8"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"PH, US welcome new initiatives on energy, agri, regional peace","publisher":"Philippine News Agency","publication_date":"2022-11-21","url":"https://www.pna.gov.ph/articles/1189030","claim":"The Philippines and the United States launched talks on a 123 agreement for civil nuclear cooperation, framed as supporting zero-emission energy and nonproliferation priorities.","locator":"November 21, 2022 report on Harris visit initiatives","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Government newswire summarizing a U.S. factsheet and the bilateral announcement tied to Marcos's meeting with Harris.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 ruler-period evidence.","ruler_attribution":"Indirect but still Marcos-admin attributable because it was a bilateral initiative launched during his term.","contrary_evidence":["The report is about initiating talks, not concluding safeguards or deploying nuclear technology.","Most of the detail is from the U.S. factsheet rather than a Marcos-specific operational order."],"lenses":["1B.3","1B.4","1B.6","1B.9"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"PH keen to partner with other countries for nuclear power dev’t","publisher":"Philippine News Agency","publication_date":"2022-11-29","url":"https://www.pna.gov.ph/articles/1189755","claim":"Philippine officials said the country was open to nuclear-energy negotiations with countries beyond the United States, and that the 123 agreement was crucial for any U.S. nuclear-export pathway.","locator":"PNRI director comments on future nuclear negotiations","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium","source_confidence_reason":"Government newswire quoting a PNRI official, useful for policy context but not a direct Marcos directive.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Exact 2022 policy context.","ruler_attribution":"Indirect administrative context rather than a direct Marcos order.","contrary_evidence":["The article attributes most of the detail to PNRI commentary, not to a newly signed Marcos decision.","It broadens the picture but does not prove any safety or command-control improvement."],"lenses":["1B.3","1B.6","1B.7","1B.9"]}

**Search and rejection summary**
I considered roughly three dozen documents across official Philippine sources, UN/IAEA treaty records, government wire coverage, and independent reporting. I kept only the documents that added something materially different about office, constraints, treaty baseline, civilian nuclear choices, or de-escalatory nuclear rhetoric.

Removed or de-prioritized items fell into these buckets:
- Duplicate coverage: multiple PCO and PNA pieces repeated the same SONA nuclear quote or the same 123-agreement launch.
- Out of period: 2023 123-agreement entry-into-force coverage, 2023 NuScale and Ultra Safe Nuclear pieces, and 2024-2026 cybersecurity/AI and nuclear-safety items were informative but not 2022 evidence.
- Weak / overbroad: generic climate, infrastructure, or science articles that did not add ruler-level existential-risk choice beyond what the SONA and treaty sources already established.
- Blocked pages: several PCO pages returned 403 or decoding errors when opened directly, so I relied on the search snippets for those rather than promoting lower-quality substitutes.
- Low-value repeats: later commentary on nuclear energy in general, without Marcos-specific choice or a 2022 timestamp, was not kept.

**Remaining gaps**
- There is no evidence that Marcos had any nuclear-weapon command, custody, launch, or command-and-control authority in 2022, because the Philippines is a non-nuclear state. That means 1B is structurally a limited-exposure chapter here.
- I did not find a 2022 Marcos-specific decision on cyber, bio, or AI with a credible catastrophic pathway. The public record is too generic to treat those domains as discriminating evidence for this ruler-period.
- I did not find a 2022 appointment record showing Marcos building a distinct nuclear-safety or existential-risk expert bench beyond the general nuclear-energy policy apparatus.
- More search would mostly refine the civilian nuclear-energy narrative, not change the core exposure assessment.

chapter_notes.2B:
Exposure gate: `limited_exposure_with_choices`. The 2022 local prior shows no internationalized conflict events or fatalities for the Philippines, so this chapter turns on diplomacy, alliance management, and maritime-dispute handling rather than wartime conduct.

**Reused evidence**
