The local structured prior already covers the baseline on killings, disappearances, repression, protests, and civil-society pressure. This pass adds ruler attribution, the early Marcos response, and 2022 accountability/protest context.

New evidence

Marcos brought a civilian academic into the security apparatus as his national security adviser.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Marcos names nat’l security adviser, DAR chief","publisher":"Philippine News Agency","publication_date":"2022-06-08","url":"https://www.pna.gov.ph/articles/1176235","claim":"Marcos appointed retired UP professor Clarita Carlos as National Security Adviser under his administration.","locator":"opening paragraph","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"State newswire reporting an announced Cabinet appointment with photo and caption context.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 ruler-period evidence, before and immediately after inauguration.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to Marcos's staffing choice.","contrary_evidence":["Appointment alone does not prove restraint or effectiveness.","Carlos later voiced policy intent, but implementation still needed corroboration."],"lenses":["3B.4","3B.5","3B.10"]}

Marcos publicly promised to protect human rights and emphasized accountability early in the transition.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Marcos vows to protect human rights","publisher":"Philippine News Agency","publication_date":"2022-06-10","url":"https://www.pna.gov.ph/articles/1176399","claim":"Marcos guaranteed that human rights would be protected during his watch and said his team was considering consultations to uphold and protect human rights.","locator":"opening paragraphs","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous state newswire quoting Marcos and the UN resident coordinator, but not a verbatim transcript.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 ruler-period evidence, just before inauguration.","ruler_attribution":"Directly about Marcos's public commitment at the transition into office.","contrary_evidence":["A vow is not implementation.","Later 2022 reporting shows continuing killings and weak accountability."],"lenses":["3B.1","3B.4","3B.10"]}

Marcos's incoming security team signaled an end to red-tagging.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"New Philippine Security Adviser Urges End to 'Red-Tagging'","publisher":"Human Rights Watch","publication_date":"2022-06-14","url":"https://www.hrw.org/news/2022/06/14/new-philippine-security-adviser-urges-end-red-tagging","claim":"Incoming national security adviser Clarita Carlos said she wanted to stop red-tagging and described it as not productive, while HRW noted the practice had been carried out through the Duterte-era task force.","locator":"opening paragraphs and quoted remarks","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Independent human-rights reporting with direct quote and institutional context.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 ruler-period evidence at the start of Marcos's term.","ruler_attribution":"Indirect but materially relevant because Carlos was Marcos's incoming security adviser and part of his chosen team.","contrary_evidence":["This is a stated intention, not proof that red-tagging stopped.","The same report says the task force had been responsible for much of the practice."],"lenses":["3B.2","3B.3","3B.4","3B.8"]}

CHR publicly backed the end-red-tagging stance and warned of its harms.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Statement of Executive Director, Atty. Jacqueline Ann de Guia, welcoming the statement of the Justice Secretary calling an end to red-tagging and respect for freedoms","publisher":"Commission on Human Rights of the Philippines","publication_date":"2022-06-18","url":"https://chr.gov.ph/statement-of-executive-director-atty-jacqueline-ann-de-guia-welcoming-the-statement-of-the-justice-secretary-calling-an-end-to-red-tagging-and-respect-for-freedoms/","claim":"CHR welcomed Justice Secretary Menardo Guevarra's call to end red-tagging and warned that labeling groups without sufficient evidence can expose them to attacks and chills free expression.","locator":"opening paragraphs and quoted statement","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official national human-rights institution statement directly describing the risk of red-tagging.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 ruler-period evidence during Marcos's transition.","ruler_attribution":"Indirect; it reflects institutions around Marcos's incoming administration, not a direct order from Marcos.","contrary_evidence":["A statement by CHR does not prove the executive branch adopted the advice.","The persistence of red-tagging later in 2022 limits any positive inference."],"lenses":["3B.2","3B.4","3B.8"]}

A permitted SONA protest dispersed, which is a small positive signal on assembly space.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Protesters on Commonwealth Ave. disperse ahead of SONA","publisher":"Philippine News Agency","publication_date":"2022-07-25","url":"https://www.pna.gov.ph/articles/1179688","claim":"Rallyists with a permit protested ahead of Marcos's first SONA and voluntarily dispersed after the police said their permit had lapsed, with some grace period allowed.","locator":"opening paragraphs and police quotation","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"State newswire reporting on a permitted protest and police handling on the day of the SONA.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 ruler-period evidence.","ruler_attribution":"Indirectly attributable through national police action under Marcos's administration; the protest itself was not Marcos-directed.","contrary_evidence":["The report shows tight policing of assembly despite permission.","A permitted protest does not prove a consistently open protest environment."],"lenses":["3B.8","3B.9"]}

CHR reminded police to show maximum tolerance during the first SONA protests.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Bongbong Marcos' first SONA: It's all about economy","publisher":"Philippine Daily Inquirer","publication_date":"2022-07-25","url":"https://newsinfo.inquirer.net/1633568/marcos-first-sona-its-all-about-economy","claim":"The Commission on Human Rights reminded police to exercise maximum tolerance during peaceful protests and warned against water cannons or firearms while monitoring SONA rallies for abuses.","locator":"paragraphs on CHR reminder and monitoring","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Independent local reporting quoting CHR guidance issued for Marcos's first SONA.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 ruler-period evidence.","ruler_attribution":"Indirect; it concerns the policing environment under Marcos rather than a personal order from him.","contrary_evidence":["The need for this warning indicates protest sensitivity and a nontrivial repression risk.","The article itself does not show whether the guidance fully worked."],"lenses":["3B.8","3B.9"]}

The transition period still produced a violent dispersal of an anti-Marcos rally.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Anti-Marcos rally inside CHR compound turns violent amid dispersal by police","publisher":"Inquirer.net","publication_date":"2022-05-25","url":"https://newsinfo.inquirer.net/1602570/anti-marcos-rally-inside-chr-compound-turns-violent-amid-dispersal-by-police","claim":"Police dispersed an anti-Marcos demonstration at the CHR compound, with officers using water cannon after a scuffle as protesters tried to proceed toward Batasang Pambansa.","locator":"opening paragraphs and police account","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous local reporting on a public protest and police response, with both police and rally narrative described.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Pre-inauguration 2022 context that shaped the transition into Marcos's term.","ruler_attribution":"Indirect; it predates Marcos's inauguration and reflects the transition environment rather than his direct orders.","contrary_evidence":["The police version is disputed within the article.","This is a single incident and not by itself proof of a nationwide pattern."],"lenses":["3B.8","3B.9","3B.10"]}

Police blocked activists from marching during the first 100 days protest.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Protest during Philippine President Ferdinand Marcos Jr's first 100 days in office","publisher":"Reuters Connect","publication_date":"2022-10-08","url":"https://www.reutersconnect.com/item/protest-during-philippine-president-ferdinand-marcos-jrs-first-100-days-in-office/dGFnOnJldXRlcnMuY29tLDIwMjI6bmV3c21sX1JDMlFXVzlTNTlDNQ?position=2&previouslyViewed=dGFnOnJldXRlcnMuY29tLDIwMjQ6bmV3c21sX1JDMkZINkE4NjkzRA","claim":"Police officers blocked Filipino activists from marching during a protest marking Marcos's first 100 days in office.","locator":"photo caption","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Reuters photo caption directly documents police obstruction of a protest in Marcos's first 100 days.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 ruler-period evidence.","ruler_attribution":"Indirectly attributable to the policing environment under Marcos's administration.","contrary_evidence":["A photo caption captures one event, not the whole protest climate.","It does not show Marcos personally ordered the blockage."],"lenses":["3B.8","3B.9"]}

Marcos rejected ICC re-entry, while Amnesty said domestic accountability remained inadequate.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Philippines: Decision to not rejoin the ICC statute will not prevent investigation","publisher":"Amnesty International","publication_date":"2022-08-10","url":"https://www.amnesty.org/en/documents/asa35/5936/2022/en/","claim":"Amnesty said the Marcos administration's decision not to rejoin the ICC would not stop the investigation into Duterte-era crimes because there was no evidence of genuine domestic investigations and prosecutions.","locator":"opening paragraph and summary","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Clear advocacy statement anchored in the administration's announced decision and Amnesty's accountability assessment.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 ruler-period evidence.","ruler_attribution":"Directly about a Marcos administration accountability choice.","contrary_evidence":["The administration argued domestic investigations were sufficient.","This concerns inherited abuses, but Marcos still chose the non-rejoin position."],"lenses":["3B.1","3B.4","3B.5","3B.10"]}

The UN human-rights committee pressed the Marcos government on a spike in extrajudicial killings.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Human Rights Committee, Summary record of the 3919th meeting, Fifth periodic report of the Philippines","publisher":"United Nations Human Rights Committee","publication_date":"2022-10-14","url":"https://docstore.ohchr.org/SelfServices/FilesHandler.ashx?enc=txj%2FuyEih8AnABECajgMJ01q1E2%2FgBqqfbuMLEwyTvmVyzuwR5yFgr4AkD3RGdI8%2FbneDJRxcUEBpr3fcDMP7w%3D%3D","claim":"A committee member said there had already been a 78 percent increase in extrajudicial killings under Marcos Jr. and asked what the state would do to stop them and end drug watch lists.","locator":"paragraphs 17-18","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official UN committee record of the state review and member questions, with direct period-specific scrutiny.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 ruler-period evidence during the Marcos administration.","ruler_attribution":"Indirect but important because it documents what UN experts were pressing the Marcos administration on during 2022.","contrary_evidence":["The record also includes the Philippine delegation's claims of reform and accountability efforts.","A committee question is not the same as a judicial finding, but it is still highly relevant scrutiny."],"lenses":["3B.1","3B.2","3B.4","3B.10"]}

Independent monitoring showed drug-war killings continued at high levels in Marcos's first months.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Drug killings under Marcos Jr. surpass those killed in last six months of Duterte","publisher":"VERA Files / UP Third World Studies Center (Dahas Project)","publication_date":"2022-12-04","url":"https://verafiles.org/articles/drug-killings-under-marcos-jr-surpass-those-killed-in-last-six-months-of-duterte","claim":"The Dahas Project data showed 152 drug-related killings in the first five months of the Marcos administration, exceeding the 149 killings recorded in the final six months of the Duterte government.","locator":"opening paragraph and first data summary","source_type":"scholarship","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"University-based monitoring data reported in an investigative outlet, with clear comparative counts.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 ruler-period evidence through Nov. 30, 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Directly relevant to Marcos's first five months in office.","contrary_evidence":["The report says Marcos adopted a different strategy, but the killings continued at a high level.","Counting methodology is monitoring-based, not official police data."],"lenses":["3B.1","3B.2","3B.6","3B.10"]}

HRW said the killings remained commonplace and impunity stayed severe.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"UN still needs to scrutinize the Philippines","publisher":"Human Rights Watch","publication_date":"2022-09-12","url":"https://www.hrw.org/news/2022/09/12/un-still-needs-scrutinize-philippines","claim":"HRW said drug-related killings remained commonplace under the Marcos administration and reported that Dahas recorded 72 drug-related killings in July and August, while only one conviction had come from more than 6,200 drug-war killings by police since 2016.","locator":"opening paragraphs and data summary","source_type":"NGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Independent human-rights monitoring with concrete comparative data and accountability context.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 ruler-period evidence.","ruler_attribution":"Partly direct, partly inherited; it documents the post-inauguration environment Marcos inherited and did not yet meaningfully reverse.","contrary_evidence":["The article partly critiques U.N. caution after Marcos took office, so it is advocacy-framed.","The figures combine inherited Duterte-era violence with Marcos-era continuation."],"lenses":["3B.1","3B.2","3B.4","3B.10"]}

The first SONA omitted a rights/accountability break with the past.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Disappointment as Marcos keeps mum on drug war, other human rights issues","publisher":"Philippine Center for Investigative Journalism","publication_date":"2022-07-26","url":"https://pcij.org/2022/07/26/disappointment-as-marcos-keeps-mum-on-drug-war-other-human-rights-issues/","claim":"PCIJ reported that Marcos's first SONA did not set any policy to undo Duterte's drug campaign and was silent on press freedom, red-tagging, and the killings of journalists and activists.","locator":"opening paragraphs and quoted Human Rights Watch response","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Investigative local reporting summarizing the SONA and human-rights reaction, though still interpretive in tone.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 ruler-period evidence.","ruler_attribution":"Directly about Marcos's first major policy address.","contrary_evidence":["An omission is not the same as an explicit rights-abuse policy.","Marcos's SONA emphasized other governance priorities instead."],"lenses":["3B.4","3B.6","3B.8","3B.10"]}

Search and rejection summary
I considered roughly 30 documents across PNA, CHR, HRW, Amnesty, OHCHR, Reuters syndications, Inquirer, PCIJ, VERA Files/UP Dahas, GMA, and a few later retrospective reports. I kept the items that actually moved a 3B assessment: office/authority, early human-rights signaling, red-tagging, protest handling, ICC/accountability, and drug-war continuation.

Removed or deprioritized items fell into these buckets:
- Duplicates: multiple stories repeated the same red-tagging remarks, the same SONA protest sequence, or the same ICC non-rejoin announcement.
- Out of period: 2023-2026 Dahas retrospectives, 2024-2026 CHR inquiries, and later commentary on the same issues were useful for search but not accepted as 2022 evidence.
- Weak or off-topic: election-campaign unity pieces, foreign-policy articles, and economic stories without a direct domestic-safety nexus.
- Blocked or partial access: some PCO and Reuters/republish pages were not practical to open cleanly, so I used accessible official or syndicated copies instead.
- Over-derivation risk: I avoided turning a single protest or a single statement into multiple source units unless the claims were materially distinct.

Remaining gaps
- Direct evidence of Marcos personally ordering police, prison, or intelligence reform in 2022 is still thin. Most of the record is staffing choices, public promises, or institutional reactions.
- I did not find strong 2022 evidence that red-tagging was measurably reduced or that any new accountability mechanism had started to work by year end.
- The public record on torture, enforced disappearance, detention conditions, and remedies under Marcos in 2022 is still sparse compared with the drug-war and protest material.
- More search would help most on vulnerable-group protection, police accountability, and whether protest policing was a local anomaly or part of a broader national pattern.

