SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"FOCAP concerned over difficulty accessing Bongbong Marcos, his supporters’ attack on journos","publisher":"GMA News Online","publication_date":"2022-05-03","url":"https://www.gmanetwork.com/news/topstories/nation/830504/focap-concerned-over-difficulty-accessing-bongbong-marcos-his-supporters-attack-on-journos/story/","claim":"FOCAP said Marcos supporters attacked journalists online and that reporters had difficulty getting clear and substantial answers during the campaign, with pro-Marcos content endangering and smearing independent journalists.","locator":"lines 115-120","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Local mainstream reporting quoting an organized press body and specific incidents.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 campaign-period evidence.","ruler_attribution":"Mostly direct to Marcos's campaign milieu; some abuse came from supporters rather than from Marcos personally.","contrary_evidence":["The report focuses on supporters and access issues, not a direct written order from Marcos.","It is evidence of the climate around the campaign, not a formal censorship mechanism."],"lenses":["4B.3","4B.6","4B.9"]}

- [CHR, NUJP urge gov't to address attacks vs. journalists](https://www.gmanetwork.com/news/topstories/nation/847830/nujp-urge-gov-t-to-address-attacks-vs-journalists/story/) shows the threat environment persisted into Marcos's first months in office.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"CHR, NUJP urge gov't to address attacks vs. journalists","publisher":"GMA News Online","publication_date":"2022-10-12","url":"https://www.gmanetwork.com/news/topstories/nation/847830/nujp-urge-gov-t-to-address-attacks-vs-journalists/story/","claim":"The CHR called on the Marcos administration to address attacks on journalists after death threats against TV5 reporters and after the killing of Percy Lapid, and the NUJP challenged the administration to translate its promise to protect journalists into action.","locator":"lines 104-123","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous reporting on CHR and NUJP reactions to concrete threats and a killing during Marcos's first months.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 ruler-period evidence.","ruler_attribution":"Partly direct and partly inherited: it documents what the Marcos administration faced and what it had not yet demonstrably fixed.","contrary_evidence":["The article shows pressure on the administration to respond, not a proven policy of repression by Marcos himself.","It is about threats and accountability rather than a formal censorship law."],"lenses":["4B.6","4B.9","4B.10"]}

- [Marcos leads presidential race amid massive disinformation](https://pcij.org/2022/05/08/marcos-presidential-elections-massive-disinformation/) is the clearest local summary of the disinformation advantage around his campaign.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Marcos leads presidential race amid massive disinformation","publisher":"Philippine Center for Investigative Journalism","publication_date":"2022-05-08","url":"https://pcij.org/2022/05/08/marcos-presidential-elections-massive-disinformation/","claim":"PCIJ reported that studies found Marcos was the main beneficiary of false or misleading content on social media and that his campaign relied on historical revisionism and narrative control.","locator":"lines 110-111 and 155-156","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Investigative local reporting synthesizing fact-checking and academic work on the campaign information environment.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 election-period evidence.","ruler_attribution":"Directly tied to Marcos's campaign advantage and media strategy.","contrary_evidence":["The article also describes a preexisting, broader disinformation ecosystem, so not all of it is uniquely attributable to Marcos.","It is still a campaign-environment claim, not a formal state censorship claim."],"lenses":["4B.2","4B.6","4B.10"]}

Search and rejection summary
I screened roughly 30 documents across official Philippine election bodies, congressional records, local investigative reporting, Reuters Institute research, press-freedom monitors, and human-rights groups. I kept the items that materially affected a 4B judgment: formal transfer, election-process legitimacy, campaign narrative control, press access, harassment, and early administration posture.

Removed or deprioritized items fell into these buckets:
- Duplicates: several articles repeated the same red-tagging, journalist access, or disinformation claims.
- Out of period: 2023 and later press-freedom or disinformation articles were informative but not 2022 target-period evidence.
- Irrelevant to 4B: foreign policy, nuclear-energy, and domestic-safety sources that do not bear on political freedom were excluded here.
- Blocked or partial access: some official pages and social posts were not cleanly fetchable; where the direct page failed, I used an accessible mirrored or reported copy instead.
- Overly generalized democracy indices: I did not re-fetch Freedom House, V-Dem, RSF, BTI, or Polity because the local prior already supplies those structured facts and the task asked not to re-fetch structured datasets.

Remaining gaps
- I did not find strong 2022 evidence that Marcos independently strengthened courts, legislatures, audit bodies, or local oversight institutions. `4B.4` remains the thinnest lens.
- I did not find direct 2022 evidence of a major institutional reform that improved political equality for excluded groups or opposition regions under Marcos personally. `4B.7` is still weak.
- The record on surveillance and digital controls is mostly indirect: campaign hostility, disinformation, and platform pressure are visible, but I did not find a Marcos-ordered surveillance or shutdown policy in 2022.
- More search would help mainly on appointment choices, formal media-policy decisions, and any concrete changes to institutional independence after the inauguration.

