**Search and rejection summary**
- I screened roughly 30-plus documents across PCO, Reuters/Reuters-syndicated reports, IMF, PSA, DOF, World Bank, LawPhil, and PNA.
- I kept 12 distinct URLs with non-duplicative claims; the retained set centers on the 2022 cabinet/economic team, Marcos's SONA fiscal and regulatory agenda, tariff relief, IMF macro assessment, PSA macro outputs, DOF debt reporting, and food-security distribution programs.
- I rejected or de-emphasized duplicate mirrors of the same SONA, repeated Reuters syndications of the same appointment story, and later follow-up pieces from 2023-2026 because they did not add target-year evidence.
- I dropped partisan or advocacy-adjacent items that mostly restated praise without new evidence, including Senate commendations and PSE praise for the economic team.
- I excluded campaign-period promise pieces from March-May 2022 unless they were later confirmed by 2022 administration actions.
- I excluded later tariff and anti-hunger follow-ups from 2023-2026 because they were outside the target year.
- I did not re-fetch structured local indicators already provided in the prior; they were used as context, not as standalone evidence citations.

**Remaining gaps**
- Direct, independent evidence on anti-corruption and capture in 2022 is still thin. I have policy-intent evidence on rightsizing, customs streamlining, and budget modernization, but not much audited implementation on favoritism or oligarchic privilege.
- Household-level distribution data for 2022 is limited. I have strong macro data and some food-access program outputs, but not a clean micro distribution picture for the Marcos period itself.
- More search would help on 2022 execution of the SONA legislative agenda, COA/audit findings, and whether customs/tax reforms were actually implemented or just announced.
- More search would also help distinguish how much of the 2022 growth and labor-market improvement was Marcos-era policy versus inherited reopening and rebound effects.

